{
"dur": 62.727,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 11.892,
"text": "The family put money into more than loans. In 1853 Nathaniel bought the Bordeaux vineyard now called Ch\u00e2teau Mouton Rothschild. In 1868 James bought Ch\u00e2teau Lafite.",
"end": 12.167
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 12.167,
"vo_start": 12.442,
"vo_end": 23.159,
"text": "They had terrible timing. A tiny insect, phylloxera, was about to destroy most of France's vineyards. The family had the money to ride it out. Many growers didn't.",
"end": 23.434
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 23.434,
"vo_start": 23.709,
"vo_end": 32.849,
"text": "They backed mines too. The London and Paris houses helped finance Cecil Rhodes in merging the South African diamond fields into De Beers in 1888.",
"end": 33.124
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 33.124,
"vo_start": 33.399,
"vo_end": 45.841,
"text": "In the 1880s the Paris branch bought into oil around Baku on the Caspian Sea, and for a while was one of the biggest players in the world oil trade, up against Rockefeller's Standard Oil.",
"end": 46.116
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 46.116,
"vo_start": 46.391,
"vo_end": 50.147,
"text": "They sold out in 1911, to Royal Dutch Shell, for shares.",
"end": 50.422
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 50.422,
"vo_start": 50.697,
"vo_end": 61.777,
"text": "And there were eccentrics. Walter Rothschild filled a museum with hundreds of thousands of specimens, rode a giant tortoise, and drove a carriage pulled by zebras to Buckingham Palace.",
"end": 62.727
}
],
